---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="70–80 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary and topics · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Abstract and academic topics: <em>make the thinking precise and visible</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Academic language should help a listener see what was observed, what is inferred, what remains uncertain, and why it matters. A longer noun does not make a claim stronger. Begin with the source and reasoning move; then choose a natural word family, collocation, level of certainty, and scope. If a compact phrase hides the agent or action, unpack it into plain English.</p>
  <div class="meta-row"><span class="tag">70–80 min core</span><span class="tag">source → reasoning move → claim</span><span class="tag">word families and collocations</span><span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span></div>
  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow"><div class="flow-step"><b>Classify</b><span>Observation, interpretation, or implication?</span></div><div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Choose family, grammar, and collocation.</span></div><div class="flow-step"><b>Calibrate</b><span>Match certainty and scope to design.</span></div><div class="flow-step"><b>Explain</b><span>Make dense ideas clear aloud.</span></div></div>
  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can distinguish observation, finding, data, variable, association, causal effect, mechanism, hypothesis, assumption, interpretation, limitation, implication, and recommendation; use high-value word families with accurate stress and word class; treat <span class="ex">evidence</span> and <span class="ex">research</span> as normally uncountable; choose natural collocations for studies, data, conclusions, assumptions, factors, variation, and implications; distinguish observed, statistically significant, and practically important results; treat formal plural and general singular <span class="ex">data</span> as real register variation; calibrate reporting verbs to study design; keep population, place, time, and measured outcome inside the claim; unpack dense nominalizations; and deliver an evidence-to-implication briefing in clear U.S. English.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered analysis, assumption, cautious reporting, evidence-bound conclusion, and a clear discourse turn before building a larger academic toolkit.">
    <h3>Reactivate five academic foundations</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Academic foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="analysis">analysis</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="assumption">assumption</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="suggest">suggest</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a qualified conclusion">a qualified conclusion</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Nevertheless">Nevertheless</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="analytical">analytical</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The noun in the family <span class="ex">analyze → ___</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="analysis" data-hint="Choose the noun naming the process or result of analyzing." data-fix="Analyze becomes analysis.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The noun in <span class="ex">assume → ___</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="assumption" data-hint="Choose the noun naming an idea accepted before it is tested." data-fix="Assume becomes assumption.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The limited results <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="suggest" data-hint="Use a cautious reporting verb that does not claim proof." data-fix="The limited results suggest a possible pattern.">choose</button> a possible pattern.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>After reading limited evidence, draw <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a qualified conclusion" data-hint="Match the conclusion's certainty and scope to the evidence." data-fix="Draw a qualified conclusion rather than a universal claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The evidence is limited. <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Nevertheless" data-hint="Introduce a supported result that remains worth considering despite the limitation." data-fix="Nevertheless, it identifies a question worth testing further.">choose</button>, it identifies a question worth testing further.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice: the same study supports several different statements</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Cedar Grove library reminder trial</h3>
    <p><b>Observation:</b> In an eight-week randomized trial, 138 of 300 adults in the email-plus-text group attended at least one booked workshop, compared with 111 of 300 in the email-only group. The observed difference was nine percentage points.</p>
    <p><b>Interpretation:</b> Random allocation supports the interpretation that the additional text reminder increased attendance within this study sample and these conditions. The result is more than a simple uncontrolled association, but it is not a universal guarantee.</p>
    <p><b>Limitation:</b> Participants had already opted into library messages, came from three branches, and booked one of eighteen workshops. The study lasted eight weeks and measured attendance, not learning, employment, satisfaction, or long-term participation.</p>
    <p><b>Implication:</b> The finding supports evaluating the reminder for longer and across more branches. The report gives no statistical test or confidence interval, so it does not establish that the difference was statistically significant. Whether nine points is practically important depends on costs, capacity, and the program's goal.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the observed difference, bounded causal interpretation, sampling and outcome limits, next-study implication, statistical evidence gap, and practical judgment.">
    <h3>Discovery check: observation, interpretation, and boundary</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Academic discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a nine-percentage-point observed difference">a nine-percentage-point observed difference</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a bounded causal interpretation within the trial">a bounded causal interpretation within the trial</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an opt-in three-branch sample measured for eight weeks">an opt-in three-branch sample measured for eight weeks</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="attendance rather than learning or employment">attendance rather than learning or employment</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a longer evaluation across more branches">a longer evaluation across more branches</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="no reported statistical test or confidence interval">no reported statistical test or confidence interval</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="proof that every adult will attend">proof that every adult will attend</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The numerical observation is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a nine-percentage-point observed difference" data-hint="Subtract 37% from 46% and name the unit accurately." data-fix="The study observed a nine-percentage-point difference.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Because allocation was randomized, the report can support <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a bounded causal interpretation within the trial" data-hint="Keep the inference inside the actual sample and conditions." data-fix="Random allocation supports a bounded causal interpretation within this trial.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The population and time boundary is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an opt-in three-branch sample measured for eight weeks" data-hint="Combine who entered, where the study occurred, and how long it lasted." data-fix="The sample consisted of opt-in adults at three branches over eight weeks.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The primary outcome was <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="attendance rather than learning or employment" data-hint="Do not replace the measured behavior with an unmeasured life outcome." data-fix="The study measured attendance, not learning or employment.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A proportionate practical implication is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a longer evaluation across more branches" data-hint="Extend measurement instead of declaring universal success." data-fix="The result supports a longer evaluation across more branches.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The phrase <span class="ex">statistically significant</span> is not licensed because the summary provides <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="no reported statistical test or confidence interval" data-hint="An observed difference alone does not provide the statistical uncertainty calculation." data-fix="The summary reports no statistical test or confidence interval.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: every abstract noun has a reasoning job</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Reasoning term</th><th>Job</th><th>Cedar Grove example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>observation / finding</td><td>report what the investigation recorded</td><td>Attendance was 46% versus 37%.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>data</td><td>recorded information used in analysis</td><td>Group assignment and attendance records</td></tr>
    <tr><td>variable</td><td>a measured or assigned feature that can differ</td><td>Reminder type and attendance</td></tr>
    <tr><td>association</td><td>a relationship between variables</td><td>Reminder condition varied with attendance.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>causal effect</td><td>a change attributed to an intervention under a credible design</td><td>The additional text increased attendance in the trial.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>mechanism</td><td>the process through which an effect may occur</td><td>The text may have made the booking easier to remember.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>hypothesis</td><td>a testable proposed explanation or prediction</td><td>Reminders may reduce forgotten bookings.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>assumption</td><td>an idea treated as true inside the reasoning</td><td>Messages reached both groups as recorded.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>interpretation</td><td>an account of what the result may mean</td><td>The added reminder improved attendance in these conditions.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>limitation</td><td>a boundary on what the design or evidence can establish</td><td>Only opt-in adults at three branches were included.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>implication</td><td>a possible consequence for knowledge or action</td><td>A longer multi-branch evaluation is justified.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>recommendation</td><td>a proposed action supported by a stated rationale</td><td>Test for six months and report uncertainty.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note"><h3>Association and causation are claims about design, not magic words</h3><p>An uncontrolled association does not by itself establish that one variable caused another. Random allocation can strengthen a causal inference because it creates comparable conditions before the intervention. Even then, check implementation, missing data, sample scope, outcome choice, and uncertainty. Say exactly what the design supports.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>A mechanism is not the same as an effect</h3><p>The attendance difference is the observed result. “The text made the booking easier to remember” is a proposed mechanism, but the trial did not measure memory. Present it as a hypothesis unless separate evidence supports it.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated finding, data, variable, association, bounded causal effect, mechanism, hypothesis, assumption, interpretation, limitation, implication, and recommendation in complete contexts.">
    <h3>Choose ten reasoning functions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Academic reasoning choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="finding">finding</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="variable">variable</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="association">association</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="causal effect">causal effect</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="mechanism">mechanism</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hypothesis">hypothesis</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="assumption">assumption</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="limitation">limitation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="implication">implication</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="recommendation">recommendation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="guarantee">guarantee</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The recorded 46% versus 37% difference is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="finding" data-hint="Name what the investigation observed before interpreting it." data-fix="The attendance difference is a finding.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Reminder type is an assigned <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="variable" data-hint="It has two study conditions that can differ." data-fix="Reminder type is an assigned variable.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>If two measured features vary together without a design that isolates cause, describe an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="association" data-hint="Name a relationship without adding a causal direction." data-fix="The evidence supports an association, not necessarily causation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Random allocation supports a bounded <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="causal effect" data-hint="Attribute the attendance difference to the reminder condition only within the trial's design and scope." data-fix="The design supports a bounded causal effect within the trial.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The proposed process “the text reduced forgotten bookings” is a possible <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="mechanism" data-hint="Name how the intervention might produce the effect." data-fix="Reduced forgetting is a proposed mechanism.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>“Reminders may reduce forgotten bookings” is a testable <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hypothesis" data-hint="It is a proposed explanation that another measure could test." data-fix="The memory explanation is a testable hypothesis.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>The analysis treats accurate message delivery as an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="assumption" data-hint="It is accepted inside the reasoning but must be checked against delivery records." data-fix="Accurate message delivery is an assumption to verify.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The opt-in sample from only three branches is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="limitation" data-hint="It restricts how widely the finding can be generalized." data-fix="The narrow opt-in sample is a limitation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">9</span><span>The possibility that libraries should test reminders longer is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="implication" data-hint="It states what the finding may mean for future action." data-fix="A longer evaluation is an implication of the finding.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">10</span><span>“Run a six-month multi-branch evaluation” is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="recommendation" data-hint="It proposes a specific action and should follow a rationale." data-fix="Run a six-month evaluation is a recommendation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build word families, countability, and collocations accurately</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Noun</th><th>Adjective</th><th>Stress movement</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>analyze</td><td>analysis</td><td>analytical</td><td><b>AN</b>alyze · a<b>NAL</b>ysis · ana<b>LYT</b>ical</td></tr>
    <tr><td>assume</td><td>assumption</td><td>assumed</td><td>as<b>SUME</b> · as<b>SUMP</b>tion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>conclude</td><td>conclusion</td><td>conclusive</td><td>con<b>CLUDE</b> · con<b>CLU</b>sion · con<b>CLU</b>sive</td></tr>
    <tr><td>interpret</td><td>interpretation</td><td>interpretive</td><td>in<b>TER</b>pret · interpre<b>TA</b>tion</td></tr>
    <tr><td>vary</td><td>variation</td><td>variable / varied</td><td><b>VAR</b>y · vari<b>A</b>tion · <b>VAR</b>iable</td></tr>
    <tr><td>signify</td><td>significance</td><td>significant</td><td><b>SIG</b>nify · sig<b>NIF</b>icance · sig<b>NIF</b>icant</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note"><h3><span class="ex">Evidence</span> and <span class="ex">research</span> are normally uncountable</h3><p>Use <span class="ex">evidence, some evidence, a piece or line of evidence, two sources of evidence</span>. Use <span class="ex">research, some research, a study, two studies, three articles</span>. In ordinary academic English, avoid <span class="cross">an evidence, evidences, a research, researches</span> when you mean separate studies or sources.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3><span class="ex">Data</span> has genuine number variation</h3><p>Formal scientific style often treats <span class="ex">data</span> as plural: <span class="ex">The data indicate...</span> General U.S. English often treats it as a mass noun: <span class="ex">The data indicates...</span> Follow the publication or institutional style and remain consistent. Do not design a one-answer item that pretends only one form exists.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Learn the noun with its grammatical partners</h3><p>Useful chunks include <span class="ex">conduct a study of, conduct research into, collect and analyze data, evaluate evidence, draw a conclusion from, question an assumption, identify a limitation, a factor contributes to, account for variation, implications for, result in</span>, and <span class="ex">result from</span>.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected accurate word classes, uncountable forms, study and data collocations, conclusion sources, assumption verbs, factor patterns, implications, and result direction.">
    <h3>Complete eleven word-family and collocation decisions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Academic form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="analysis">analysis</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="analytical">analytical</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a piece of evidence">a piece of evidence</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="two studies">two studies</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="conducted a study of">conducted a study of</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="analyzed the attendance data">analyzed the attendance data</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="draw a conclusion from">draw a conclusion from</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="questioned the assumption">questioned the assumption</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="contributing factor">contributing factor</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="implications for">implications for</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="resulted from">resulted from</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an evidence">an evidence</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The noun naming careful examination is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="analysis" data-hint="Choose the noun, not the adjective." data-fix="The analysis examined attendance patterns.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The adjective before <span class="ex">approach</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="analytical" data-hint="Choose the adjective meaning based on systematic analysis." data-fix="The team adopted an analytical approach.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>One attendance record is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a piece of evidence" data-hint="Count a piece or source because evidence is normally uncountable." data-fix="One attendance record is a piece of evidence.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Two separate investigations are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="two studies" data-hint="Count studies rather than using researches for separate projects." data-fix="The review compared two studies.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The researchers <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="conducted a study of" data-hint="Use conduct a study of before the topic." data-fix="The researchers conducted a study of workshop attendance.">choose</button> workshop attendance.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="analyzed the attendance data" data-hint="Use analyze directly before the information examined." data-fix="The team analyzed the attendance data.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Do not <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="draw a conclusion from" data-hint="Use draw a conclusion from before the source." data-fix="Do not draw a conclusion from one unverified record.">choose</button> one unverified record.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The audit <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="questioned the assumption" data-hint="Use question as a verb before an idea previously accepted." data-fix="The audit questioned the assumption that every message arrived.">choose</button> that every message arrived.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">9</span><span>Reminder timing may be one <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="contributing factor" data-hint="Name one influence among several, not a complete cause." data-fix="Reminder timing may be one contributing factor.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">10</span><span>The result has <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="implications for" data-hint="Use the established noun plus for collocation before future action." data-fix="The result has implications for future reminder design.">choose</button> future reminder design.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">11</span><span>The delivery failure <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="resulted from" data-hint="Use result from to name the source or cause of an outcome." data-fix="The delivery failure resulted from an outdated phone number.">choose</button> an outdated phone number.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Calibrate the claim instead of choosing a permanently cautious verb</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Reporting verbs form a design-dependent scale</h3><p><span class="ex">Reports, records, finds</span> describe what the source states or observes. <span class="ex">Is associated with</span> names a relationship without assigning cause. <span class="ex">Suggests, indicates, is consistent with</span> support interpretations at different strengths determined by context. <span class="ex">Supports a causal effect</span> requires a design capable of isolating an intervention. <span class="ex">Demonstrates</span> and <span class="ex">establishes</span> are stronger and need correspondingly strong evidence. <span class="ex">Proves</span> fits mathematical proof and a few tightly defined claims; it is usually too absolute for one applied study.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3><span class="ex">Significant</span> has everyday and statistical meanings</h3><p>In everyday academic prose, <span class="ex">a significant cost</span> can mean an important or substantial cost. <span class="ex">Statistically significant</span> is a technical conclusion based on a statistical model and threshold. It does not automatically mean the effect is large, useful, or important. An observed difference is not automatically statistically significant, and a statistically significant result is not automatically practically important.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Scope is part of accuracy</h3><p>Keep the population, setting, time, comparison, and measured outcome visible: <span class="ex">Among adults who opted into messages at three branches, the additional text increased eight-week workshop attendance.</span> Do not silently expand <span class="ex">attendance</span> into learning or <span class="ex">these participants</span> into every adult.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Unpack dense nouns when the actor disappears</h3><p><span class="ex">The implementation of the evaluation produced an identification of variation</span> is grammatically possible but needlessly opaque. Prefer <span class="ex">The library evaluated the program and identified variation among branches.</span> Use nominalization when it creates a useful concept, not when it hides who did what.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched observation, association, bounded causal inference, cautious interpretation, strong evidence, statistical uncertainty, practical importance, explicit scope, plain agents, and qualified recommendations.">
    <h3>Choose ten claim-strength and clarity boundaries</h3>
