  <h2>Sound natural: stress the family and chunk the reasoning</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Academic speech is not a race through long words. Keep word-family stress accurate, separate the source from the interpretation, reduce function words, and place the final nucleus on the new finding or boundary. A short pause can make the evidence-to-claim relationship easier to hear.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Spoken line</th><th>Connection</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>We <b>AN</b>alyzed the data. / The a<b>NAL</b>ysis found a nine-point difference.</td><td>stress moves across the word family</td><td>process, then finding</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The results INdicate / a MODEST attendance difference.</td><td><span class="ex">results indicate</span> links across /s ɪ/</td><td>calibrated result</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One FACtor in / the attendance difference / may be TIMing.</td><td><span class="ex">factor in</span> links /ˈfæktərɪn/</td><td>one possible influence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>This has impliCA tions for / future deSIGN.</td><td><span class="ex">for</span> commonly reduces to /fər/</td><td>practical consequence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The finding applies / to THESE participants / in THIS trial.</td><td>scope forms its own corrective chunks</td><td>population and setting limit</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Do not stress every abstract noun equally. Compare <span class="ex">The DATA show a difference</span> with <span class="ex">The data show a NINE-point difference</span>. The second version treats the existence of data as shared and makes the size new. In conversation, <span class="ex">The study found...</span> may be clearer than a dense phrase such as <span class="ex">The investigation's identification of...</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read analysis stress shift, linked results-indicate, linked factor-in, reduced implications-for, and contrastive scope listening check" data-success-message="Correct. You recovered word-family stress, a calibrated finding, a possible factor, a practical implication, and explicit scope from five connected U.S. English lines.">
