  <h2>Read the source before accepting the abstract summary</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Fictional Cedar Grove methods and results file</h3>
    <p>From January 15 through March 10, Cedar Grove Public Library studied reminders for eighteen free evening skills workshops at three branches. Six hundred adults who had already opted into library messages and booked a workshop were randomly assigned after booking. Three hundred received the usual email reminder. Three hundred received the same email plus one text message twenty-four hours before the workshop.</p>
    <p>The primary outcome was whether a participant attended at least one booked workshop during the eight-week period. In the email-plus-text group, 138 of 300 participants attended, or 46%. In the email-only group, 111 of 300 attended, or 37%. The observed difference was nine percentage points. The report did not include a statistical test, confidence interval, message-delivery audit, or analysis of workshop type.</p>
    <p>The text service cost $1,260 for setup and messages during the trial. The study did not report staff time, cost per additional attendee, or whether workshop capacity affected attendance. It did not measure learning, employment, satisfaction, accessibility, repeat attendance after March 10, or people who had not opted into messages.</p>
    <p>Random assignment supports a causal interpretation of the reminder difference within the study sample if implementation occurred as described. Missing delivery and workshop-type information remains a limitation. The sample and outcomes do not support a claim about all adults, all libraries, or learning. A longer evaluation should include more branches, delivery auditing, uncertainty estimates, accessibility measures, and learning outcomes.</p>
  </div>
