    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> Present reduced forgetting as a mechanism that still needs testing.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>One possible mechanism is that the text reduced forgotten bookings, although memory was not measured.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> State the population and outcome boundary.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The finding applies to workshop attendance among opt-in adults at three branches, not to learning or every resident.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> Draw a practical implication that builds evidence.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The result has implications for a longer multi-branch evaluation with delivery, uncertainty, accessibility, and learning measures.</strong></p></div>
  </div></div>

  <h2>Practice analysis without exposing private data or beliefs</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>You never need to discuss a real diagnosis, disability, academic result, income, employment review, immigration case, political affiliation, legal dispute, private research dataset, or confidential workplace evidence. Use Cedar Grove or a tutor-created study. The skill is accurate reasoning and clear language, not disclosure of personal outcomes.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Reasoning map:</b> Sort ten statements from a fictional report into observation, interpretation, mechanism, assumption, limitation, implication, and recommendation. Defend every label.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Family switch:</b> Explain one idea using a verb, noun, and adjective from <span class="ex">analyze, assume, conclude, interpret, vary</span>, or <span class="ex">significant</span>. Move the stress accurately.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Design switch:</b> Change a report from uncontrolled observation to random allocation, then remove the delivery audit. Adjust the strongest defensible reporting verb each time.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Scope switch:</b> Change the population, site, period, or measured outcome. Reformulate the claim so no hidden generalization remains.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Clarity edit:</b> Find three abstract nouns in a dense sentence. Keep the useful concept, but restore an agent and action wherever the nominalization hides who did what.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Final production: deliver a two-minute evidence-to-implication briefing</h3><p>Use Cedar Grove or a new fictional source. State the research question, population, setting, time, comparison, intervention or exposure, and primary outcome. Report one exact finding. Distinguish association from causal effect according to the design. Propose a mechanism and label it as tested or untested. Identify one assumption, two limitations, one implication, and one recommendation.</p><p>Use at least eight target collocations and four complete word families. Include normally uncountable <span class="ex">evidence</span> and <span class="ex">research</span>, one consistent choice for <span class="ex">data</span>, one everyday use of <span class="ex">significant</span>, and one accurately supported or explicitly unsupported statistical-significance statement. Give a dense written version, then unpack it into clear spoken U.S. English with accurate family stress and scope chunks. Your listener changes the design or outcome; recalibrate immediately.</p><p><b>Listener check:</b> Can the listener identify the source, observation, variables, design strength, interpretation, mechanism, assumption, limitation, statistical boundary, practical significance, scope, implication, recommendation, word-family choices, and agents behind the abstract nouns?</p></div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved reasoning functions, word class, collocation, design-sensitive certainty, statistical boundaries, practical importance, scope, and clear reformulation."><h3>Seven final decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The selected blocks and short duration limit how widely the results can be generalized." data-hint="The B2 assessment requires the sample and time limits, not rejection of every finding." data-fix="The selected blocks and short duration limit how widely the results can be generalized."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which limitation matches the B2 assessment's research reading?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The selected blocks and short duration limit how widely the results can be generalized.">The selected blocks and short duration limit how widely the results can be generalized.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A short pilot automatically proves a universal urban result.">A short pilot automatically proves a universal urban result.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Because the sample is limited, no temperature was ever measured.">Because the sample is limited, no temperature was ever measured.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Continue with a broader, better-designed trial while treating the current result as provisional." data-hint="Use the current evidence to justify stronger testing, not permanent certainty." data-fix="Continue with a broader, better-designed trial while treating the current result as provisional."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which implication matches the B2 assessment's evidence strength?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Continue with a broader, better-designed trial while treating the current result as provisional.">Continue with a broader, better-designed trial while treating the current result as provisional.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Adopt the intervention everywhere because one pilot proves complete causation.">Adopt the intervention everywhere because one pilot proves complete causation.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Ignore all observed results because the design has limitations.">Ignore all observed results because the design has limitations.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Random assignment supports a causal interpretation within this trial, but the opt-in sample limits generalization." data-hint="Recognize the design's internal strength and external scope limit together." data-fix="Random assignment supports a bounded causal interpretation, while the opt-in sample limits generalization."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence evaluates Cedar Grove's design accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Random assignment supports a causal interpretation within this trial, but the opt-in sample limits generalization.">Random assignment supports a causal interpretation within this trial, but the opt-in sample limits generalization.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Random assignment proves the result for every adult and every outcome.">Random assignment proves the result for every adult and every outcome.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="An opt-in sample means random assignment provides no useful comparison.">An opt-in sample means random assignment provides no useful comparison.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The report observed a nine-percentage-point difference but did not report whether it was statistically significant." data-hint="Separate the visible numerical result from an unreported uncertainty analysis." data-fix="The report observed nine percentage points but did not report statistical significance."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which statement handles statistical significance responsibly?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The report observed a nine-percentage-point difference but did not report whether it was statistically significant.">The report observed a nine-percentage-point difference but did not report whether it was statistically significant.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every nine-point difference is statistically significant and important.">Every nine-point difference is statistically significant and important.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Statistical significance can be read directly from any two percentages without analysis.">Statistical significance can be read directly from any two percentages without analysis.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The study provides two useful pieces of evidence and has implications for future research." data-hint="Use a counting unit with evidence and for after implications." data-fix="The study provides two useful pieces of evidence and has implications for future research."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which sentence controls countability and collocation?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The study provides two useful pieces of evidence and has implications for future research.">The study provides two useful pieces of evidence and has implications for future research.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The research provides two evidences and implications on future.">The research provides two evidences and implications on future.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="An evidence has an implication into researches.">An evidence has an implication into researches.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The library evaluated the reminder and found variation among branches." data-hint="Name the agent and actions instead of stacking abstract nouns." data-fix="The library evaluated the reminder and found variation among branches."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which version makes the dense idea clearest?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The library evaluated the reminder and found variation among branches.">The library evaluated the reminder and found variation among branches.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The evaluation implementation had a variation identification realization.">The evaluation implementation had a variation identification realization.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Variation evaluation implementation of identification was had.">Variation evaluation implementation of identification was had.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Use abstract vocabulary when it identifies a real reasoning function, and unpack it when it hides the source, actor, action, or limit." data-hint="Precision and clarity matter more than length or formality." data-fix="Keep useful concepts visible and unpack needless density."><p class="quiz-question">7. Which principle produces clear academic English?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Use abstract vocabulary when it identifies a real reasoning function, and unpack it when it hides the source, actor, action, or limit.">Use abstract vocabulary when it identifies a real reasoning function, and unpack it when it hides the source, actor, action, or limit.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Replace every verb with the longest available noun.">Replace every verb with the longest available noun.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Choose formal words before checking what the source measured.">Choose formal words before checking what the source measured.</button></div></div>
  </div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p></div>

  <h2>Reflect: can the source still be seen through the vocabulary?</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Explain the reasoning architecture</h3><p>Complete these lines aloud: <span class="ex">The source directly records...</span> <span class="ex">The reasoning function is...</span> <span class="ex">The word family is...</span> <span class="ex">The natural collocation is...</span> <span class="ex">The design supports...</span> <span class="ex">The mechanism is tested / untested...</span> <span class="ex">Statistical uncertainty is...</span> <span class="ex">Practical importance depends on...</span> <span class="ex">The claim applies to...</span> <span class="ex">The abstract noun hides / preserves...</span></p><p>Then change the design, remove the uncertainty analysis, widen the population, or replace the outcome. Reformulate the strongest accurate claim and explain why it changed.</p><p><a href="#next-day-retrieval">Return tomorrow for the five-item retrieval check</a> before rereading the lesson.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Next use:</b> Read one serious public research summary. Extract one observation, one design feature, one interpretation, one limitation, and one implication. Rewrite one dense sentence in clear agent-action form.</div>
  <section id="next-day-retrieval" data-lesson-extension="Next-day reasoning function, evidence countability, bounded causal inference, statistical-practical distinction, and plain-language reformulation retrieval"><h2>Next-day retrieval</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div><p>Return without rereading. Reconstruct function, family, collocation, claim strength, scope, and plain meaning in five new fictional contexts.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You retained reasoning functions, evidence countability, design-bounded causation, statistical versus practical significance, and plain agent-action reformulation."><h3>Five delayed decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The recorded difference is a finding; the claim that reduced confusion caused it is an interpretation with an untested mechanism." data-hint="Separate what the source observed from what the analyst thinks may explain it." data-fix="The difference is a finding; the proposed reduced-confusion mechanism is an interpretation that still needs testing."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence separates observation from explanation?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The recorded difference is a finding; the claim that reduced confusion caused it is an interpretation with an untested mechanism.">The recorded difference is a finding; the claim that reduced confusion caused it is an interpretation with an untested mechanism.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every interpretation is automatically an observed mechanism.">Every interpretation is automatically an observed mechanism.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A finding and a hypothesis are always identical.">A finding and a hypothesis are always identical.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The review found two studies and several useful pieces of evidence." data-hint="Count studies and pieces, not research and evidence themselves." data-fix="The review found two studies and several useful pieces of evidence."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence controls the normally uncountable nouns?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The review found two studies and several useful pieces of evidence.">The review found two studies and several useful pieces of evidence.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The review found two researches and several evidences.">The review found two researches and several evidences.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The review found an evidence and a researchs.">The review found an evidence and a researchs.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Random allocation supports an effect on appointment attendance within the sampled clinics, not a universal effect on health." data-hint="Preserve the assigned comparison, measured attendance outcome, sampled setting, and excluded health claim." data-fix="Random allocation supports a bounded attendance effect within the sampled clinics."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which causal claim matches a randomized appointment-reminder trial?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Random allocation supports an effect on appointment attendance within the sampled clinics, not a universal effect on health.">Random allocation supports an effect on appointment attendance within the sampled clinics, not a universal effect on health.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Random allocation proves every reminder improves every health outcome.">Random allocation proves every reminder improves every health outcome.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A randomized comparison can only describe an uncontrolled association.">A randomized comparison can only describe an uncontrolled association.</button></div></div>
